<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1960</h1><p class="ref-lede">1960 in world history: 35 events, 6 births, 18 deaths, 2 works, 19 states founded and 13 that came to an end. 336 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a><a href="/years/1957">1957</a><a href="/years/1958">1958</a><a href="/years/1959">1959</a><a href="/years/1960" aria-current="page">1960</a><a href="/years/1961">1961</a><a href="/years/1962">1962</a><a href="/years/1963">1963</a><a href="/years/1964">1964</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Greensboro sit-in begins at a Woolworth's lunch counter</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Four Black students sat at a whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina, and stayed after being refused service. Sit-ins spread across the South, and Woolworth's desegregated the counter that July.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alberto Korda photographs Che Guevara</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> memorial for victims of a ship explosion, Korda captured Guevara's gaze in a photograph later titled 'Guerrillero Heroico'. It became one of the most reproduced images in the world.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-21">21 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Police kill 69 protesters at Sharpeville</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">South African police fired on an unarmed crowd protesting against the pass laws, killing 69 people. The government banned the ANC and PAC, and 21 March is now Human Rights Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Brasília is inaugurated as Brazil's capital</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Built in under four years to plans by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> as capital. It was meant to draw development into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s interior.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-1">1 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Soviet missile downs a US U-2 spy plane</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A U-2 piloted by Francis Gary Powers was shot down near Sverdlovsk. Washington's initial cover story collapsed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> produced the pilot, wrecking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> summit two weeks later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-9">9 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">FDA says it will approve the first contraceptive pill</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The US Food and Drug Administration announced it would license Enovid for contraception. The pill gave women far greater control over fertility and transformed family life.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-16">16 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Theodore Maiman operates the first laser</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At Hughes Research Laboratories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Maiman produced a pulse of red laser light from a ruby crystal. Lasers went on to transform medicine, communications and industry.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-23">23 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ben-Gurion announces the capture of Adolf Eichmann</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Israel's prime minister told the Knesset that Eichmann, a principal organiser of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, had been found and was in Israeli custody. Mossad agents had seized him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Chad becomes independent from France</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> gained independence, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tombalbaye">François Tombalbaye</a> as its first president. It was one of 17 African countries to win independence in 1960, the 'Year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-1">1 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nigeria gains independence from Britain</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s most populous country, became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-tafawa-balewa">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</a> as prime minister. It was one of seventeen African states to win independence in 1960, the 'Year of Africa'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-orleans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ruby Bridges enters an all-white school in New Orleans</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ruby Bridges became the first African American child to attend the formerly whites-only William Frantz Elementary School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> school desegregation crisis. Norman Rockwell painted her in The Problem We All Live With (1964).</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Sharpeville massacre took place on 21 March 1960 in the township of Sharpeville, south of Johannesburg, when police fired on a crowd protesting against the pass laws in a campaign led by the Pan Africanist Congress, killing 69 people.</p></div></li></ol></section>
